"On the Importance of a Date, or, Decolonizing the Anthropocene | ACME: An International Journal for Critical Geographies (https://acme-journal.org/index.php/acme/article/view/1539)\nciteturn4view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://acme-journal.org/index.php/acme/article/view/1539\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 77\nL0: cite0†Skip to main content cite1†Skip to main navigation menu cite2†Skip to site footer L1: \nL2: Open Menu\nL3:   * cite3†Current L4:   * cite4†Forthcoming L5:   * cite5†Archives L6:   * cite6†Announcements L7:   * cite7†About L8:     * cite7†About the Journal L9:     * cite8†Editorial Team L10:     * cite9†How We Are Organized L11:     * cite10†Copyright Notice L12:     * cite11†Reprint Rights L13:     * cite12†Privacy Statement L14:     * cite13†Contact L15:   * cite14†Submissions L16:     * cite15†Submission Formats L17:     * cite16†Author Submission Guidelines L18:     * cite17†Special Issues & Themed Issues Submission Guidelines L19:     * cite18†Submission Preparation Checklist L20:   * cite19†Account L21: \nL22: cite20†Search L23:   * cite21†Register L24:   * cite19†Login L25: # On the Importance of a Date, or, Decolonizing the Anthropocene\nL26: \nL27: ## Authors\nL28: \nL29:   * Heather Davis\nL30:   * Zoe Todd\nL31: \nL32: ## DOI:\nL33: \nL34: cite22†https://doi.org/10.14288/acme.v16i4.1539†doi.org L35: \nL36: ## Keywords:\nL37: \nL38: Anthropocene, decolonization, Indigenous philosophy, colonialism\nL39: ## Abstract\nL40: This article argues for the importance of including Indigenous knowledges into contemporary discussions of the Anthropocene. We argue that a start date coincident with colonization of the Americas would more adequately open up these conversations.\nL41: In this, we draw upon multiple Indigenous scholars who argue that the Anthropocene is not a new event, but is rather the continuation of practices of dispossession and genocide, coupled with a literal transformation of the environment, that have been at work for the last five hundred years. Further, the Anthropocene continues a logic of the universal which is structured to sever the relations between mind, body, and land.\nL42: In dating the Anthropocene from the time of colonialization, the historical and ideological links between the events would become obvious, providing a basis for the possibility of decolonization within this framework.\nL43: ## Downloads\nL44: \nL45:   * cite23†PDF L46: \nL47: ## Published\nL48: \nL49: 2017-12-20\nL50: \nL51: ## How to Cite\nL52: \nL53: Davis, H., & Todd, Z. (2017). On the Importance of a Date, or, Decolonizing the Anthropocene. ACME: An International Journal for Critical Geographies, 16(4), 761–780. https://doi.org/10.14288/acme.v16i4.1539\nL54: \nL55: [Button: More Citation Formats ]\nL56: \nL57:   * cite24†APA L58:   * cite25†Chicago L59:   * cite26†Harvard L60:   * cite27†MLA L61: \nL62: Download Citation\nL63: \nL64:   * cite28†Endnote/Zotero/Mendeley (RIS) L65:   * cite29†BibTeX L66: \nL67: ## Issue\nL68: \nL69: cite30†Vol. 16 No. 4 (2017) L70: \nL71: ## Section\nL72: \nL73: Research\nL74: ## License\nL75: \nL76: Authors agree to publish their articles in ACME under the Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial-NoDerivatives 4.0 International License. To read and review the agreement, cite31†click here†creativecommons.org . In line with fair attribution and proper permissions, note any copyrights of materials cited in your paper. Do not use materials that are not fair use without express written consent.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n1539 (https://warwick.ac.uk/fac/arts/english/currentstudents/postgraduate/masters/modules/en9b5worldlitanthropocene/davis_and_todd-decolonising_the_anthropocene.pdf)\nciteturn4view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 20; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://warwick.ac.uk/fac/arts/english/currentstudents/postgraduate/masters/modules/en9b5worldlitanthropocene/davis_and_todd-decolonising_the_anthropocene.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 734\nL0@P0: \nL1@P0:  Published with Creative Commons licence: Attribution–Noncommercial–No Derivatives\nL2@P0: On the Importance of a Date, or Decolonizing\nL3@P0: the Anthropocene1\nL4@P0: Heather Davis\nL5@P0: heathermargaret@gmail.com\nL6@P0: Zoe Todd\nL7@P0: zoe.todd@gmail.com\nL8@P0: Abstract\nL9@P0: This article argues for the importance of including Indigenous knowledges into\nL10@P0: contemporary discussions of the Anthropocene. We argue that a start date\nL11@P0: coincident with colonization of the Americas would more adequately open up these\nL12@P0: conversations. In this, we draw upon multiple Indigenous scholars who argue that\nL13@P0: the Anthropocene is not a new event, but is rather the continuation of practices of\nL14@P0: dispossession and genocide, coupled with a literal transformation of the\nL15@P0: environment, that have been at work for the last five hundred years. Further, the\nL16@P0: Anthropocene continues a logic of the universal which is structured to sever the\nL17@P0: relations between mind, body, and land. In dating the Anthropocene from the time\nL18@P0: of colonialization, the historical and ideological links between the events would\nL19@P0: \nL20@P0: 1\nL21@P0:  This paper was originally written in June, 2016 as the members of the Anthropocene Working\nL22@P0: Group were deciding upon the status and appropriate date for the proposed epoch. It was meant as\nL23@P0: an intervention into their decision-making process, in the hopes that they might place the ‘golden\nL24@P0: spike,’ or start date, at 1610. As such, a draft of this article was circulated amongst the Working\nL25@P0: Group members that summer. Although the Working Group’s work has come to an end, the\nL26@P0: Anthropocene has not yet been officially adopted and the start date has yet to be decided upon. We\nL27@P0: hope that this article might serve as a continued intervention to show the political efficacy of\nL28@P0-1: placing the GSSP at 1610. On the Importance of a Date, or Decolonizing the Anthropocene 762\nL29@P1: become obvious, providing a basis for the possibility of decolonization within this\nL30@P1: framework.\nL31@P1: Keywords\nL32@P1: Anthropocene; decolonization; Indigenous philosophy; colonialism\nL33@P1: Introduction\nL34@P1: The Anthropocene is here. These were the headlines following the\nL35@P1: conclusion of the Working Group on the Anthropocene in August 2016. The group\nL36@P1: recommended the adoption of the Anthropocene as a new geological epoch to the\nL37@P1: International Geological Congress (IGC). The approval process will take an\nL38@P1: estimated two more years and still requires ratification from three academic bodies\nL39@P1: (Phys.org 2016), but it will most likely be named an official epoch following the\nL40@P1: Holocene. This recommendation clearly has political implications beyond the\nL41@P1: bounds of the discipline of geology, for stating that we are living in a geologic\nL42@P1: epoch determined by the detritus, movement, and actions of humans is itself a\nL43@P1: political act. Andrew Barry and Mark Maslin have recently argued that Paul\nL44@P1: Crutzen himself – the atmospheric chemist who, alongside ecologist Eugene\nL45@P1: Stoermer, popularized the term ‘Anthropocene’ in its current iteration (Crutzen and\nL46@P1: Stoermer 2000) – recognizes that the concept of the Anthropocene “had evident\nL47@P1: political and ethical implications” (Barry and Maslin 2016, np). If instantiated as an\nL48@P1: epoch, the political stakes of this claim rest in part on the placement of the ‘golden\nL49@P1: spike’ or the Global Boundary Stratotype Section and Point (GSSP),2 the date at\nL50@P1: which geologists decide that the Anthropocene would begin. In other words, it is\nL51@P1: not only the decision of whether or not the current geological time frame should be\nL52@P1: considered the Anthropocene, but the question of when that opens up political\nL53@P1: consequences far beyond the IGC. A number of dates have been proposed, from the\nL54@P1: birth of agriculture to the first steam engine, but the working group has\nL55@P1: recommended the mid-twentieth century as the optimal boundary. The mid\u0002twentieth century is the working group’s preferred start date due to the fact that so\nL56@P1: many measurable anthropogenic changes began at that moment. Referred to as the\nL57@P1: ‘great acceleration’ (Steffen et al. 2015), these changes are now written into the\nL58@P1: geologic strata and can be seen globally. 3\nL59@P1: \nL60@P1: 2\nL61@P1:  This is a marker placed in geological strata to identify the beginning of the new epoch (University\nL62@P1: of Leicester 2016)\nL63@P1: 3 The geologic markers that coincide with a start date for the Anthropocene in the 1950s include\nL64@P1-2: carbon dioxide levels, mass extinctions, and the widespread use of petrochemicals including plastic, ACME: An International Journal for Critical Geographies, 2017, 16(4): 761-780 763\nL65@P2: Since we are not geologists, we cannot evaluate the dates for their\nL66@P2: stratigraphic accuracy or scientific merit. However, we would like to propose that\nL67@P2: this dating of the Anthropocene misses a valuable opportunity for evaluating the\nL68@P2: concept and opening it up beyond its current Eurocentric framing. Instead, we\nL69@P2: argue that placing the golden spike at 1610, or from the beginning of the colonial\nL70@P2: period, names the problem of colonialism as responsible for contemporary\nL71@P2: environmental crisis. If the Anthropocene is already here, the question then\nL72@P2: becomes, what can we do with it as a conceptual apparatus that may serve to\nL73@P2: undermine the conditions that it names? One could object that by dating the\nL74@P2: Anthropocene to colonialism we are undoing the critical and creative work that has\nL75@P2: been done to name the problem of colonialism and its power differentials because\nL76@P2: the Anthropocene, as a term, erases these questions of power. Indeed, many people\nL77@P2: in the humanities have pointed out the failure of the Anthropocene, as a concept, to\nL78@P2: adequately account for power relations. 4 Instead, all humans are equally implicated\nL79@P2: under the sign of the ‘anthopos.’ But rather than abandon the term because of these\nL80@P2: connections, we feel that the Anthropocene betrays itself in its name: in its\nL81@P2: reassertion of universality, it implicitly aligns itself with the colonial era. By\nL82@P2: making the relations between the Anthropocene and colonialism explicit, we are\nL83@P2: then in a position to understand our current ecological crisis and to take the steps\nL84@P2: needed to move away from this ecocidal path.\nL85@P2: Our contention here is that the Anthropocene, if explicitly linked to the\nL86@P2: beginnings of colonization, would at least assert it as a critical project that\nL87@P2: understands that the ecocidal logics that now govern our world are not inevitable or\nL88@P2: ‘human nature’, but are the result of a series of decisions that have their origins and\nL89@P2: reverberations in colonization. From this place, we can begin the project of\nL90@P2: decolonizing the Anthropocene. However, without recognizing that from the\nL91@P2: beginning, the Anthropocene is a universalizing project, it serves to re-invisibilize\nL92@P2: the power of Eurocentric narratives, again re-placing them as the neutral and global\nL93@P2: perspective. By linking the Anthropocene with colonization, it draws attention to\nL94@P2: the violence at its core, and calls for the consideration of Indigenous philosophies\nL95@P2: and processes of Indigenous self-governance as a necessary political corrective,\nL96@P2: alongside the self-determination of other communities and societies violently\nL97@P2: impacted by the white supremacist, colonial, and capitalist logics instantiated in the\nL98@P2: origins of the Anthropocene.\nL99@P2: The story we tell ourselves about environmental crises, the story of\nL100@P2: humanity’s place on the earth and its presence within geological time determines\nL101@P2: \nL102@P2: but the most convincing marker is the plutonium left from radioactivity left from the detonation of\nL103@P2: atomic bombs. 4\nL104@P2:  This problem of universalization is the reason why others have called for the Anthropocene to be\nL105@P2: re-named the Capitalocene (Haraway 2015; Malm 2013; Moore 2015), Eurocene (Grove 2016) or\nL106@P2-3: White Supremacy Scene (Mirzoeff 2016).On the Importance of a Date, or Decolonizing the Anthropocene 764\nL107@P3: how we understand how we got here, where we might like to be headed, and what\nL108@P3: we need to do. We make the case for colonialism as the start date of the\nL109@P3: Anthropocene for two reasons: the first is to open up the geologic questions and\nL110@P3: implications of the Anthropocene beyond the realm of Western and European\nL111@P3: epistemology to think with Indigenous knowledges from North America; the\nL112@P3: second is to make a claim that to use a date that coincides with colonialism in the\nL113@P3: Americas allows us to understand the current state of ecological crisis as inherently\nL114@P3: invested in a specific ideology defined by proto-capitalist logics based on\nL115@P3: extraction and accumulation through dispossession – logics that continue to shape\nL116@P3: the world we live in and that have produced our current era. We focus on North\nL117@P3: America because that is the place that both of us currently live and have grown up\nL118@P3: in. We recognize that to be taken seriously by the IGC we would need to propose a\nL119@P3: date that could be seen as having a global impact, but we refuse to write from an\nL120@P3: un-embodied or universal position, and by writing from where we know, we hope\nL121@P3: we can connect to other histories that are beyond the scope of this article and to\nL122@P3: incite further reflection from other parts of the world. We see this article as one\nL123@P3: part of a broader collaborative intervention into the current universalist (read:\nL124@P3: Eurocentric) discourses of the Anthropocene, hopefully making space for further\nL125@P3: discussions about the Anthropocene and its impacts from other oppressed and\nL126@P3: marginalized communities. Further, in making this claim, we are aligning ourselves\nL127@P3: with the date that Simon Lewis and Mark Maslin propose, what they call the ‘Orbis\nL128@P3: Spike’ of 1610.5 We also recognize that it is perhaps not as important to come up\nL129@P3: with a fixed or universal date for the beginning of the Anthropocene as it is to\nL130@P3: understand the explicit and implicit political investments of this term and its\nL131@P3: consequences. We intend for this argument to be constructive in situating the\nL132@P3: concept of the Anthropocene as one that we hope can traverse the natural and\nL133@P3: social sciences and humanities. To accomplish this, we take the fact of the\nL134@P3: Anthropocene as given, as recommended by the IGC working group, however, we\nL135@P3: seek to build upon existing evidence to shift the terms of this discourse to open up\nL136@P3: ways to think through the cyclical nature of what we are now collectively\nL137@P3: experiencing. To provide context, we are a team consisting of a white settler\u0002Canadian who grew up in Métis, Ojibway/Chippewa territory in Pinawa, Manitoba\nL138@P3: and Algonquin/Anishnabek territory in Deep River, Ontario, Canada; and an\nL139@P3: Indigenous (Métis) woman who grew up in amiskwaciwâskahin (Edmonton),\nL140@P3: Alberta, Canada.\nL141@P3: \nL142@P3: 5\nL143@P3:  The “Orbis spike,” is what Lewis and Maslin call the decrease in atmospheric carbon dioxide that\nL144@P3: measured the genocide of Indigenous peoples. The word refers to the Latin for world, because after\nL145@P3: 1492 human relations became intensively globalized in ways different from previous inter-regional\nL146@P3-4: or inter-continental relations. ACME: An International Journal for Critical Geographies, 2017, 16(4): 761-780 765\nL147@P4: These decisions regarding the start date of the Anthropocene are\nL148@P4: particularly pertinent for the discipline of geography, from physical to\nL149@P4: environmental to human geography, straddling both the sciences and humanities\nL150@P4: (Barry and Maslin, 2016). The Anthropocene, like the etymology of geography,\nL151@P4: describes the literal writing of the earth: geo-graphy. The Anthropocene is the\nL152@P4: epoch under which ‘humanity’ – but more accurately, petrochemical companies\nL153@P4: and those invested in and profiting from petrocapitalism and colonialism – have\nL154@P4: had such a large impact on the planet that radionuclides, coal, plutonium, plastic,\nL155@P4: concrete, genocide and other markers are now visible in the geologic strata. Noel\nL156@P4: Castree describes it as a ‘promiscuous concept’ that is particularly relevant to\nL157@P4: geography because “it describes not merely the ‘human impact’ on the nonhuman\nL158@P4: world but also the folding of human activity into earth-surface systems such that it\nL159@P4: becomes in some sense endogenous to those systems” (2015, np). As Mark Maslin\nL160@P4: notes “in many ways the Anthropocene is the perfect conceptualisation of what\nL161@P4: ‘geography’ as a subject has always represented” (2016, 8). The Anthropocene, and\nL162@P4: thinking with geology, has increasingly been taken up by critical geographers who\nL163@P4: are interested in thinking with the human, and human politics, through the\nL164@P4: consequences into deep time. This can be seen in recent essays by Kathryn Yusoff\nL165@P4: whose work examines ‘geologic life’ through the figures of fossils and fossil fuels\nL166@P4: (2013), geologic subjects (2015), and the new narratives that the Anthropocene\nL167@P4: creates, or, in her terms, an anthropogenesis (2016). Nigel Clark argues that the\nL168@P4: Anthropocene has become a moment to think with earth processes themselves, as a\nL169@P4: kind of ‘speculative geophysics’ (2012, 260). Elizabeth Johnson and Harlan\nL170@P4: Morehouse, together with contributions from six other geographers (2014), reflect\nL171@P4: on the intervention of the Anthropocene for the discipline of geography and the\nL172@P4: attending possibilities for pedagogy and political action. Geography, then, is\nL173@P4: particularly well suited to thinking through the difficulties of both geologic time\nL174@P4: and its socio-political implications.\nL175@P4: In what follows, we begin by outlining the political consequences of the\nL176@P4: proposed date for the beginning of the Anthropocene – the mid-twentieth century –\nL177@P4: stating the reasons why we feel 1610 would be a more efficacious date. We then\nL178@P4: move on to show how the logic of the Anthropocene is already entwined with\nL179@P4: colonialism, and end with a discussion of how Indigenous knowledges should be\nL180@P4: productively engaged to disrupt and undo these universalizing and violent logics.\nL181@P4: Dates and consequences\nL182@P4: The date that the Anthropocene Working Group has recommended is the\nL183@P4: mid-twentieth century. Steffen et al. (2015) argue that the onset of the\nL184@P4: Anthropocene coincides with the measurable impacts of the ‘great acceleration’\nL185@P4: upon the Earth System (often settling on 1964). While this date is quite convincing\nL186@P4: because it groups together all of the obvious horrors of the twentieth century, from\nL187@P4-5: the atomic bomb, to petrochemicals, to overconsumption and waste (particularly of On the Importance of a Date, or Decolonizing the Anthropocene 766\nL188@P5: non-biodegradable substances such as plastics and concrete), due to the globality of\nL189@P5: these problems, this date doesn’t significantly differentiate between countries,\nL190@P5: ideologies, or ways of life. Instead, we are all grouped together under the sign of\nL191@P5: the ubiquitous hockey stick graphs – the graphs that show the increase of various\nL192@P5: human activities indicative of the ‘great acceleration’ – where McDonald’s,\nL193@P5: international tourism, population and ocean acidification bind the whole of\nL194@P5: humanity together into one horrifying reality.6 While this may be an accurate\nL195@P5: depiction of the past seventy years, it does little to register the very real differences\nL196@P5: between peoples, governments, and geographies in their complicity with these\nL197@P5: processes. Further, the diffuse, global and overwhelming problems associated with\nL198@P5: these figures leaves little analysis for political action. It is much easier to simply\nL199@P5: despair of our present circumstances. We also wonder, not being geologists, how it\nL200@P5: would be possible to predict the impacts of an epoch that will supposedly last so far\nL201@P5: into the future with so little geologic data to go on. When geologic epochs typically\nL202@P5: last more than three million years, it seems rather pre-emptive to be deciding this in\nL203@P5: advance and with the stratigraphic records of less than a century.\nL204@P5: We are not the first to propose colonialism, and its compatriot, settler\nL205@P5: colonization, as the start date of the Anthropocene. Geographers Simon L. Lewis\nL206@P5: and Mark A. Maslin advanced two hypotheses for the possible golden spike, one of\nL207@P5: which is the Columbian Exchange, which they date to 1610. They propose 1610 for\nL208@P5: two reasons. The first is that the amount of plants and animals that were exchanged\nL209@P5: between Europe and the Americas during this time drastically re-shaped the\nL210@P5: ecosystems of both of these landmasses, evidence of which can be found in the\nL211@P5: geologic layer by way of the kinds of biomass accumulated there. The second\nL212@P5: reason, which is a much more chilling indictment against the horrifying realities of\nL213@P5: colonialism, is the drop in carbon dioxide levels that can be found in the geologic\nL214@P5: layer that correspond to the genocide of the peoples of the Americas and the\nL215@P5: subsequent re-growth of forests and other plants. Lewis and Maslin note that in\nL216@P5: 1492 there were between 54 to 61 million peoples in the Americas and by 1650\nL217@P5: there were 6 million. They argue that “On the basis of the movement of species,\nL218@P5: atmospheric CO2 decline and the resulting climate-related changes within various\nL219@P5: stratigraphic records, we propose that the 7–10 p.p.m. dip in atmospheric CO2 to a\nL220@P5: low point of 271.8 p.p.m. at 285.2 m depth of the Law Dome ice core 75, dated\nL221@P5: 1610 (615 yr; refs 75, 76), is an appropriate GSSP marker” (2015, 175). In making\nL222@P5: this claim, they understand that the implications move far beyond the geologic\nL223@P5: realm. As they write:\nL224@P5: The Orbis spike implies that colonialism, global trade and coal\nL225@P5: brought about the Anthropocene. Broadly, this highlights social\nL226@P5: concerns, particularly the unequal power relationships between\nL227@P5-6: 6 For examples of these graphs see Syvitski (2012); and Steffen et. al. (2011). ACME: An International Journal for Critical Geographies, 2017, 16(4): 761-780 767\nL228@P6: different groups of people, economic growth, the impacts of\nL229@P6: globalized trade, and our current reliance on fossil fuels. The\nL230@P6: onward effects of the arrival of Europeans in the Americas also\nL231@P6: highlights a long-term and large-scale example of human actions\nL232@P6: unleashing processes that are difficult to predict or manage. (2015,\nL233@P6: 177, our emphasis)\nL234@P6: Here, they recognize that such a proposal will impact how we understand\nL235@P6: human actions on the environment, and more generally the human-environment\nL236@P6: relation itself, as they explicitly acknowledge that this kind of differential and\nL237@P6: brutal power “unleashes processes that are difficult to predict or manage.” They\nL238@P6: write that the “formal definition of the Anthropocene makes scientists arbiters, to\nL239@P6: an extent, of the human–environment relationship, itself an act with consequences\nL240@P6: beyond geology.” (2015, 171).\nL241@P6: Geologists and other scientists will fight over these markers in scientific\nL242@P6: language, seeking traces of carbon dioxide that index the worst offenses of\nL243@P6: European empires which rent and violated the flesh,7 bodies and governance\nL244@P6: structures of Indigenous and other sovereign peoples in the name of gold, lumber,\nL245@P6: trade, land and power. But the terms to some extent have already been foreclosed\nL246@P6: by the discipline of geology. The use of ‘evidence’ here in Lewis and Maslin’s\nL247@P6: work is a particular kind of tracer. Evidence does not, generally, entail the fleshy\nL248@P6: stories of kohkoms (the word for grandmother in Cree) and the fish they fried up\nL249@P6: over hot stoves in prairie kitchens to feed their large families. As Todd points out:\nL250@P6: “Evidence generally precludes the flash of a school of minnows in the clear prairie\nL251@P6: lakes I intimately knew as a child, or the succulent white fish my stepdad caught\nL252@P6: for us from the Red Deer River when I was growing up” (2016). But these fleshy\nL253@P6: philosophies and fleshy bodies are precisely the stakes of the Anthropocene, as the\nL254@P6: Anthropocene has exacerbated existing social inequalities and power structures and\nL255@P6: divided people from the land with which they and their language, laws, and\nL256@P6: livelihoods are entwined. The stories we will tell about the origins of the\nL257@P6: Anthropocene implicate how we understand the relations we have with our\nL258@P6: surrounds. In other words, the naming of the Anthropocene epoch and its start date\nL259@P6: have implications not just for how we understand the world, but this understanding\nL260@P6: will have material consequences, consequences that affect bodies and land.\nL261@P6: Severing mind from body and land: The problem of the universal\nL262@P6: As many others have pointed out, the term Anthropocene itself is\nL263@P6: problematic because it fails to make the kinds of differentiations between world\nL264@P6: views, economies, and systems of power that we are trying to untangle here (Malm\nL265@P6: \nL266@P6: 7 This particular notion of flesh and land is drawn from the work of Vanessa Watts (2013), as\nL267@P6-7: explored in the following section.On the Importance of a Date, or Decolonizing the Anthropocene 768\nL268@P7: 2013; Moore 2015; Haraway 2016; Mirzoeff 2016). Further, the ways in which the\nL269@P7: Anthropocene as a term has been used by Crutzen and Stoermer similarly continues\nL270@P7: this unthinking ideology of divorcing thought (and by implication, humans) from\nL271@P7: other relations. In their seminal article that introduced the term Anthropocene to the\nL272@P7: written canon, simply titled “The Anthropocene,” Crutzen and Stoermer (2000: 17-\nL273@P7: 18) rely upon the concept of the ‘noösphere’ to articulate their position. They\nL274@P7: define the noösphere as “the world of thought, to mark the growing role played by\nL275@P7: mankind’s [sic] brainpower and technological talents in shaping its own future and\nL276@P7: environment” (2000, 17), a concept they credit to P. Teilhard de Chardin and E. Le\nL277@P7: Roy (2000, 17). The noösphere places thought above the biosphere and geosphere,\nL278@P7: and is framed as a teleological progression that follows the development of the\nL279@P7: earth’s geological features and biota, as demonstrated by de Chardin’s writings on\nL280@P7: the concept. In reflecting on the progression of human influence upon the globe\nL281@P7: itself, de Chardin argues:\nL282@P7: we must enlarge our approach to encompass the formation, taking\nL283@P7: place before our eyes and arising out of this factor of hominization,\nL284@P7: of a particular biological entity such as has never before existed on\nL285@P7: earth – the growth, outside and above the biosphere, of an added\nL286@P7: planetary layer, an envelope of thinking substance, to which, for the\nL287@P7: sake of convenience and symmetry, I have given the name of the\nL288@P7: Noosphere. (2004, 151)\nL289@P7: This conceptualization assumes that the biosphere cannot, in and of itself,\nL290@P7: constitute an ‘envelope of thinking substance’, which contradicts the work of\nL291@P7: biosemiotics (Bateson 1972, Kohn 2013) and the thousands of years of philosophy\nL292@P7: of many Indigenous peoples (Cruikshank 2005, Watts 2013, Vine Jr. 1997,\nL293@P7: Qitsialuk 1998, Bawaka Country et al. 2015, de la Cadena 2010, Povinelli 1995,\nL294@P7: Povinelli 2016). Drawing on this concept of a ‘thinking layer’ emergent in the\nL295@P7: earth’s processes, Crutzen and Stoermer offer the following thoughts on collective\nL296@P7: work necessary to address the Anthropocene:\nL297@P7: To develop a world-wide accepted strategy leading to sustainability\nL298@P7: of ecosystems against human induced stresses will be one of the\nL299@P7: great future tasks of mankind [sic], requiring intensive research\nL300@P7: efforts and wise application of the knowledge thus acquired in the\nL301@P7: noösphere, better known as knowledge or information society. An\nL302@P7: exciting, but also difficult and daunting task lies ahead of the global\nL303@P7: research and engineering community to guide mankind [sic] towards\nL304@P7: global, sustainable, environmental management. (2000, 15)\nL305@P7: The noösphere, while a generative category, which Crutzen and Stoermer\nL306@P7: credit with their thinking on the Anthropocene itself, replicates a Euro-Western\nL307@P7: division of mind/thought from land when it is framed as the business of ‘research\n"
